"(PDF) Indigenous Climate Change Studies: Indigenizing Futures, Decolonizing the Anthropocene (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/314114488_Indigenous_Climate_Change_Studies_Indigenizing_Futures_Decolonizing_the_Anthropocene)\nciteturn24view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/314114488_Indigenous_Climate_Change_Studies_Indigenizing_Futures_Decolonizing_the_Anthropocene\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1174\nL0: Article PDF Available\nL1: \nL2: # Indigenous Climate Change Studies: Indigenizing Futures, Decolonizing the Anthropocene\nL3: \nL4:   * March 2017\nL5:   * cite0†English Language Notes 55(1-2)\nL6: \nL7: DOI:cite1†10.1215/00138282-55.1-2.153†doi.org L8: \nL9: Authors:\nL10: \nL11: cite2†Kyle Powys Whyte L12: \nL13:   * cite3†University of Michigan L14: \nL15: cite4†Image†i1.rgstatic.net L16: \nL17: cite5†Download full-text PDF cite6†Read full-text L18: ## Abstract\nL19: [A more updated version of this paper is also in researchgate with a PDF] Indigenous and allied scholars, knowledge keepers, scientists, learners, change-makers, and leaders are creating a field to support Indigenous peoples’ capacities to address anthropogenic (human-caused) climate change.\nL20: Indigenous studies often reflect the memories and knowledges that arise from Indigenous peoples’ living heritages as societies with stories, lessons, and long histories of having to be well-organized to adapt to seasonal and inter-annual environmental changes. At the same time, our societies have been heavily disrupted by colonialism, capitalism, and industrialization. As a Potawatomi scholar-activist working on issues Indigenous people face with the U.S.\nL21: settler state, I perceive at least three key themes reflected across the field that suggest distinct approaches to inquiries into climate change: 1. Anthropogenic (human-caused) climate change is an intensification of environmental change imposed on Indigenous peoples by colonialism. 2. Renewing Indigenous knowledges, such as traditional ecological knowledge, can bring together Indigenous communities to strengthen their own self-determined planning for climate change. 3.\nL22: Indigenous peoples often imagine climate change futures from their perspectives (a) as societies with deep collective histories of having to be well-organized to adapt environmental change and (b) as societies who must reckon with the disruptions of historic and ongoing practices of colonialism, capitalism, and industrialization. In engaging these themes, I will claim, at the end, that Indigenous studies offer critical, decolonizing approaches to how to address climate change.\nL23: The approaches arise from how our ways of imagining the future guide our present actions. The article is forthcoming in English Language Notes.\nL24: cite7†Image: ResearchGate Logo L25: \nL26: Discover the world's research\nL27: \nL28:   * 25+ million members\nL29:   * 160+ million publication pages\nL30:   * 2.3+ billion citations\nL31: cite8†Join for free L32: \nL33: Public Full-text 1\nL34: \nL35: Content uploaded by cite2†Kyle Powys Whyte L36: \nL37: Author content\nL38: \nL39: All content in this area was uploaded by Kyle Powys Whyte on Feb 28, 2017\nL40: \nL41: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL42: \nL43: 1\nL44: \nL45: Indigenous Climate Chang e Studies: Indigenizing Futures, Decolonizing the Anthropocene\nL46: \nL47: Kyle Whyte\nL48: \nL49: Forthcoming 2017 in English Language Notes.\nL50: Introduction\nL51: \nL52: Indigenous and allied scholars, knowledge keepers, scientists, learners, change-makers,\nL53: \nL54: and leaders are creating a field to support Indigenous peoples’ capacities to address\nL55: \nL56: anthropogenic (human-caused) climate change. Provisionally, I call it Indigenous climate change\nL57: \nL58: studies (Indigenous studies, for short, in this essay). The studies involve many types of work,\nL59: \nL60: including Indigenous climate resiliency plans, such as the Salish-Kootenai Tribe’s Climate\nL61: Change Strategic Plan that includes sections on “Culture” and “Tribal Elder Observations,”\nL62: \nL63: policy documents, such as the In uit Petition expressing “the right to be cold,” conferences, such\nL64: \nL65: as “Climate Changed: Reflections on Our Past, Present and Future Situation,” organized by the\nL66: \nL67: Indigenous Peoples Climate Change Working Group, and numerous declarations and academic\nL68: \nL69: papers, from the Mandaluyong Declaration of the Global Conference on Indigenous Women,\nL70: Climate Change and REDD+ to a special issue of the scientific journal Climatic Change devoted\nL71: \nL72: to Indigenous peoples in the U.S. context.i\nL73: \nL74: Indigenous studies often reflect the m emories and knowledges that arise from Indigenous\nL75: \nL76: peoples’ living heritages as societies with stories, lessons, and long histories of having to be well-\nL77: \nL78: organized to adapt to seasonal and inter-annual environmental changes. At the same time, our\nL79: societies h ave been heavily disrupted by colonialism, capitalism, and industrialization. Regarding\nL80: \nL81: Indigenous peoples in the Arctic, Callison writes that climate change is “Understood as an\nL82: \nL83: emergent form of life… climate change presents the need for excavation and re assessment of\nL84: \nL85: what a recognition of climate change portends for those who have endured a century of immense\nL86: \nL87: cultural, political and environmental changes.”ii Indigenous studies, then, arise from memories,\nL88: knowledges, histories, and experiences of oppression that differ from many of the nonindigenous\nL89: \nL90: scientists, environmentalists, and politicians who are prominent in the framing of the issue of\nL91: \nL92: climate change today.\nL93: \nL94: As a Potawatomi scholar-activist working on issues Indigenous people face with the U.S.\nL95: \nL96: settler state, I perceive at least three key themes reflected across the field that suggest distinct\nL97: \nL98: approaches to inquiries into climate change:\nL99: 1. Anthropogenic (human-caused) climate change is an intensification of environmental\nL100: \nL101: change imposed on Indigenous peoples by colonialism.\nL102: \nL103: 2. R enewing Indigenous knowledges, such as traditional ecological knowledge, can bring\nL104: \nL105: together Indigenous communities to strengthen their o wn self-determined planning for\nL106: \nL107: climate change.\nL108: \nL109: 3. Indigenous peoples often imagine climate change futures from their perspectives (a) as\nL110: \nL111: societies with deep collective h istories of having to be well-organized to adapt\nL112: environmental change and (b) as societies who must reckon wi th the disruptions of\nL113: \nL114: historic and ongoing practices of colonialism, capitalism, and industrialization.\nL115: \nL116: In engaging these themes, I will claim, at the end, that Indigenous studies offer critical,\nL117: \nL118: decolonizing approaches to how to addres s climate change. The approaches arise from how our\nL119: \nL120: ways of imagining the future guide our present actions.\nL121: \nL122: 2\nL123: \nL124: Back to the Future: Climate Change as Intensified Colonialism\nL125: Colonialism refers to a form of domination in which at least one society seeks to exploit\nL126: \nL127: some set of benefits believed to be found in the territory of one or more other societies, from farm\nL128: \nL129: land to precious minerals to labor. Exploitation can occur through military invasion, slavery, and\nL130: \nL131: settlement. Colonialism oft en paved the way for the expansion of capitalism, or an economic\nL132: \nL133: ideology based on wage-labor that prioritizes growth in monetary profits for the owners of assets\nL134: as the underlying focus, incentive, and purpose of major human social endeavors.iii\nL135: \nL136: Together, c olonialism and capitalism then laid key parts of the groundwork for\nL137: \nL138: industrialization—or carbon-intensive economics—which produce the drivers of anthropogenic\nL139: \nL140: climate change, from massive deforestation for commodity agriculture to petrochemical\nL141: \nL142: technologies that burn fossil fuels for energy. The colonial invasion that began centuries ago\nL143: caused anthropogenic environmental change s that rapidly disrupted many Indigenous peoples,\nL144: \nL145: including deforestation, pollution, modification of hydrological cycles, and the amplification of\nL146: \nL147: soil-use and terraforming for particular types of farming, grazing, transportation, and residential,\nL148: \nL149: commercial and government infrastructure.\nL150: \nL151: Colonially-induced environmental changes altered the ecological conditions that\nL152: \nL153: supported Indigenous peoples’ cultures, health, economies, and political self-determination.\nL154: While Indigenous peoples, as any society, have long histories of adaptin g to change, colonialism\nL155: \nL156: caused changes at such a rapid pace that many Indigenous peoples became vulnerable to harms,\nL157: \nL158: from health problems related to new diets to erosion of their cultures to the destruction of\nL159: \nL160: Indigenous diplomacy, to which they were not as suscep tible prior to colonization. Indigenous\nL161: \nL162: peoples often understand their vulnerability to climate ch ange as an in tensification of colonially-\nL163: induced environmental changes.\nL164: \nL165: Scientific syntheses, including the U.S. National Climate Assessment and\nL166: \nL167: Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change Fifth Assessment reports, provide evidence that\nL168: \nL169: anthropogenic climat e change affects Indigenous peoples earlier and more severely than other\nL170: \nL171: populations. Indigenous peoples, for example, are already among the first “climate refugees” in\nL172: \nL173: regions such as the Arctic or Pacific where sea-level rise is occurring.iv Climate chang e affects\nL174: the integrity of Indigenous cultures and economies as the habitats change for species such as\nL175: \nL176: salmon that are important to Indigenous cultures, health, and economies.v\nL177: \nL178: Shifting habitats and climate-induced displacement have implications for Indi genous self-\nL179: \nL180: determination. They can offset agreements with nations over designated harvesting areas, such as\nL181: \nL182: treaties, that are difficult to renegotiate with more powerful nation-state parties who are heavily\nL183: influenced by corporations and constituencies of citizens who are largely ignorant about\nL184: \nL185: Indigenous peoples. Or they can throw Indigenous peoples into bureaucratic processes of\nL186: \nL187: emergency management in which Indigenous peoples’ voices are silenced by states, corporations,\nL188: \nL189: and local governments.vi\nL190: \nL191: Indigenous scholars discuss climate vulnerability as an intensification or intensified\nL192: \nL193: episode of colonialism. Wildcat claims that Indigenous climate relocation today is part of three\nL194: removals occurring as part of U.S. colonial, capitalist, and industrial expansion. The first two\nL195: \nL196: removals were “geographic” (displacement, e.g. Trail of Tears and the forced occupation of\nL197: \nL198: reservations), and “social” and “psycho-cultural” (such as through removal of children to\nL199: \nL200: boarding schools). Now,\nL201: \nL202: As ice sheets and glaciers melt permafrost thaws, and seacoasts and riverbanks erode in\nL203: \nL204: the near and circumpolar arctic, peoples indigenous to these places will be forced to\nL205: move, not as a result of something their Native lifeways produced, but because the most\nL206: \nL207: technologica lly advanced societies on the planet have built their modern lifestyles on a\nL208: \nL209: carbon energy foundation….vii\nL210: \nL211: 3\nL212: \nL213: For Wildcat, the immediacy of climate refugees is like the experience of déjà vu given that\nL214: \nL215: relocation and displacement are part of the history of colo nially-induced environmental changes\nL216: \nL217: that harmed Indigenous peoples. Hence scholars such as Kimmerer can claim that, “Once again,\nL218: we are in a situation of forced climate change adaptation.”viii\nL219: \nL220: Colonially-driven environmental change destroyed ecosystems on wh ich Indigenous\nL221: \nL222: peoples relied, boxed Indigenous peoples into small reservations that were fractions of their\nL223: \nL224: original territories, or simply displaced Indigenous peoples from their homelands to new\nL225: \nL226: ecosystems. Boarding schools forced Indigenous peoples to adopt English as their primary\nL227: language, thereby erasing the knowledges encoded within their own languages about how to live\nL228: \nL229: in relation to certain ecological conditions; Indigenous students had to adopt heterosexual and\nL230: \nL231: patriarcha l gender norms that demor alized and disenfranchised Indigenous girls, women and two-\nL232: \nL233: spirit persons. The U.S. forced Indigenous peoples to take on corporate government str uctures\nL234: \nL235: that incentivized Tribal government leaders to depend on and buy into extractive industries and\nL236: other capitalist enterprises (today, gaming is one of them but so is the coal industry).\nL237: \nL238: Through each of these practices of colonialism, Indigenous peoples witnessed the away-\nL239: \nL240: migrat ion of their nonhuman relatives. Kimmerer writes that “Like the displaced farmers of\nL241: \nL242: Bangladesh fleeing rising sea levels, maples will become climate refugees. To survive they must\nL243: \nL244: migrate northward to find homes at the boreal fringe. Our energy policy is forcing them to leave.\nL245: They will be exiled from their homelands for the price of cheap gas.”ix Mastak et al. see\nL246: \nL247: colonialism “as the literal planting and displanting of peoples, animals, and plants—as inscribing\nL248: \nL249: a domination into blood and soil…”x Away-migration also occurs in a “psycho-cultural” sense, as\nL250: \nL251: Wildcat calls it, when people lose customs, protocols, skill-sets, and identities (e.g. animal clan\nL252: \nL253: identities in some Tribes) related to particular plants, animals, insects, and ecosystems.\nL254: Indigenous studies, then, seek to understand vulnerability to climate change as an\nL255: \nL256: intensification colonialism. Chief, in work spanning several collaborations she is part of, analyzes\nL257: \nL258: the Pyramid Lake Paiute Tribe’s (PLPT) vulnerability to climate stressors in relation to their\nL259: \nL260: identitie s as Kuyuidokado/Kooyooee Tukadu, or cui-ui (fish) eaters whose relationship to the fish\nL261: \nL262: has been eroded, on a cyclical, though intensified, basis, by the Derby Dam built some 100 years\nL263: ago, high demand for water by settlers, and settler-caused environment al changes that exacerbate\nL264: \nL265: droughts.xi So climate change is related to settlement and it is the actions of settlement that\nL266: \nL267: opened up PLPT territories for the development of cities such as Reno, Nevada.\nL268: \nL269: Marino and Maldonado discuss how c limate change is an intensification of colonialism\nL270: \nL271: which opened up territories for settlements and forced some Indigenous peoples to relocate.\nL272: Marino, working with the Kigiqitamiut people in the Village of Shishmaref, Alaska, writes that\nL273: \nL274: “Previous flexibility to environmental shifts and unexpected hazards allowed the communi ty to\nL275: \nL276: adapt to abrupt changes.” Yet now a colonially-driven “relatively immobile infrastructure and\nL277: \nL278: development requires people to stay in place in order to carry out their daily lives.”xii Maldonado\nL279: \nL280: shows the vulnerability to sea level rise that is forcing the displacement of the Isle de Jean\nL281: Charles Band of Biloxi-Chitimacha-Choctaw Indians arises from a number of colonial factors tied\nL282: \nL283: to energy and agriculture including dredging canals, cutting oil and ga s pipelines, constructing\nL284: \nL285: dikes and levees, damming the Mississippi river, and large agricultural developments.xiii\nL286: \nL287: The intensified déjà vu experience of climate change engages some of the most critical\nL288: \nL289: issues Indigenous peoples face today, gender being one of them. C limate change impacts affect\nL290: Indigenous women more acutely, in many cases, while colonial policies for addressing climate\nL291: \nL292: change devalue the leadership of Indigenous women.xiv Moreover, Sweet claims that that “With\nL293: \nL294: warming temperatures and melting ice comes greater accessibility to the [Arctic] region, leading\nL295: \nL296: to more outside influences and more p otential human security threats,” including sex\nL297: \nL298: trafficking.xv Oil production fields, such as the Bakken production field in North Dakota, form\nL299: “man camps” for laborers that attract violent sex trafficking of Indigenous persons.xvi Of course,\nL300: \nL301: 4\nL302: \nL303: as a large literature in Indigenous gender studies shows, colonial domination and gender\nL304: \nL305: violence/oppression are of a piece.xvii Climate change, then, is both a gendered form of colonial ly\nL306: \nL307: imposed environmental change, and another intensified episode of colonialism that opens up\nL308: \nL309: Indigenous territories for capitalism and industrialization that occurs through gender violence.\nL310: In the studies just referenced, Indigenous persons and allies examine climate change less\nL311: \nL312: as a future trend, and more as the experience of going back to the future. For anthropoge nic\nL313: \nL314: climate change is an intensified repetition of anthropogenic environmental change inflicted on\nL315: \nL316: In digenous peoples via colonial practices that facilitated capitalist industrial expansion. The same\nL317: \nL318: colonial practices and policies that opened up Indigenous territories for deforestation and\nL319: extractive industries are the ones that m ake adaptation difficult for Indigenous peoples today.\nL320: \nL321: Anthropogenic climate change makes Indigenous territories more accessible and\nL322: \nL323: Indigenous peoples more vulnerable to harm, just as did laws, policies, boarding schools, and the\nL324: \nL325: like in previous episode s of colonization. A rising number of scholars, such as Cameron, Stuhl,\nL326: \nL327: Haalbloom and Natcher, are adamant that the analysis of Indigenous climate vulnerability cannot\nL328: occur in the absence of the history and present practices colonialism and capitalism in Indigenous\nL329: \nL330: homelands.xviii\nL331: \nL332: Renewing Relatives: Indigenous Knowledges and Climate Change\nL333: \nL334: Indigenous knowledges, in the simplest terms, refer to systems of monitoring, recording,\nL335: \nL336: communicating, and learning about the relationships among humans, nonhuman plan ts and\nL337: \nL338: animals, and ecosystems that are required for any society to survive and flourish in particular\nL339: ecosystems which are subject to perturbations of various kinds. Indigenous knowledges range\nL340: \nL341: from how ecological information is encoded in words and gramm ars of Indigenous languages, to\nL342: \nL343: protocols of mentorship of elders and youth, to kin-based and spiritual relationships with plants\nL344: \nL345: and animals, to memories of environmental change used to draw lessons about how to adapt to\nL346: \nL347: similar changes in the future.xix In digenous peoples see their knowledges as containing important\nL348: insights about how to negotiate today’s environmental issues; they often see the renewal of their\nL349: \nL350: knowledge systems as a significant strategy for achieving successful adaptation planning.\nL351: \nL352: Sak akabira, in her work with Iñupiat communities in the arctic, discusses how people live\nL353: \nL354: according to relationships of moral reciprocity with whales, an animal they depend on\nL355: \nL356: economically, culturally, and for health. The connection is so intimate Sakakabira calls it\nL357: “cetaceousnes” (whale-consciousness). While Iñupiat whale knowledge provides practical\nL358: \nL359: information on whale lifecycles that facilitate hunting and other practices that secure human\nL360: \nL361: benefits from whales, the knowledge also brings people together to respond to climate change.\nL362: \nL363: For example, climate change is experienced through changes in the availability of the whale\nL364: \nL365: tissue used for traditional drum membranes. Whereas historically drum ceremonies expressed the\nL366: whales’ invitations to bring people together, climate induced disruptions in whale cycles have\nL367: \nL368: been associated with a resurgence in drumming cere monies in some communities. The\nL369: \nL370: ceremonies now express humans’ invitation for whales to come back to reciprocal relations (with\nL371: \nL372: humans).xx\nL373: \nL374: N orgaard and Reed seek to renew Karuk knowledge, especially burning practices, as a\nL375: \nL376: basis for bringing the community members together to address climate change today—a goal they\nL377: call “knowledge sovereignty.” Reed views “climate change as a strategic opportu nity not only for\nL378: \nL379: Tribes to retain cultural practices and return traditional management practices to the landscape,\nL380: \nL381: but for all land managers to remedy inappropriate ecological actions, and for enhanced and\nL382: \nL383: successful collaboration in the face of collectiv e survival.”xxi Through rekindling traditional\nL384: \nL385: burning connected to many human, plant, fish, and animal interactions, the Karuk climate change\nL386: \nL387: 5\nL388: strategy renews Karuk knowledge to convene the community members themselves and improve\nL389: \nL390: the basis for collaborating with nonindigenous parties.\nL391: \nL392: The St. Regis Mohawk Change Plan, spurred by the leadership of Arquette and Benedict,\nL393: \nL394: is organized entirely from the human relationships with plants, animals, spiritual beings, and\nL395: \nL396: ecosystems of their Thanksgiving Address and that are part of Mohawk knowledge of how to be\nL397: good environmental stewards. The plan’s sections are divided into “The People, Mother Earth,\nL398: \nL399: The Waters, The Fish, Small Plants and Grasses, The Berries, Three Sisters, Medicine Herbs,\nL400: \nL401: Animals, Trees, The Bir ds, The Four Winds, The Thunderers, Grandmother Moon, The Sun, the\nL402: \nL403: Starts, the Four Beings, the Creator”.xxii Each section in the vulnerability analysis begins with a\nL404: \nL405: story and a description of the cultural and historical significance of the relationships, fo llowed by\nL406: comparisons between observed changes and scientific information about climate change.\nL407: \nL408: Solutions to climate adaption in the report involve the continued rene wal of the relationships,\nL409: \nL410: whether through education or stewardship practices, to mobilize community members to take\nL411: \nL412: action to address climate change.\nL413: \nL414: In McNeeley’s work with Koyukon people of Koyukuk-Mid dle Yukon region in the\nL415: \nL416: Arctic, o ne of the issues was that the state of Alaska, in order to cope with the consequences of\nL417: climate change, was imposing hunting regulations on moose that would restrict Indigenous\nL418: \nL419: subsistence harvesting. The collaborators constructed, using Koyukon knowledge of their\nL420: \nL421: seasonal round, a seasonal wheel that shows their understanding of seasonality. The Seasonal\nL422: \nL423: Round original sketch was hand drawn by a Koyukon youth after a community focus group.\nL424: \nL425: Subsequently, different iterations were reviewed by elders and com munity members. The\nL426: seasonal wheel, which illustrates numerous human relationships, terrestrial and aquatic plants and\nL427: \nL428: animals, and technologies, demonstrates that shifting the moose hunting season later so as to\nL429: \nL430: correspond with the Indigenous view of seas onality make more sense than the date proposed by\nL431: \nL432: state and federal regulators.xxiii\nL433: \nL434: Renewing Indigenous knowledges can bring together Indigenous communities to\nL435: \nL436: strengthen their self-determined planning for climate change. In the cases just described renewing\nL437: knowledges involved renewing relationships with humans and nonhumans and restoring\nL438: \nL439: reciprocity among the relatives (i.e. the parties to the relationships). I call this process renewing\nL440: \nL441: relatives, as it involves both restoring persisting relationships that are part of longstanding\nL442: \nL443: Indigenous heritages but also creating new relationships that support Indigenous peoples’\nL444: \nL445: mobilizing to address climate change. While Indigenous knowledges obviously have useful\nL446: information about the nature of ecological changes, it is perhaps more interesting to explore how\nL447: \nL448: renewing Indigenous knowledges serves the motivation of people and communities to address\nL449: \nL450: climate change.\nL451: \nL452: Of course, many Indigenous persons are understandably concerned that climate scientists\nL453: \nL454: will intentio nally or naively clamor around Indigenous communities to exploit the information\nL455: \nL456: Indigenous knowledges mi ght possess that could fill in gaps in climate science research. Hardison\nL457: and Williams, in their work at the Tulalip Tribe’s Treaty Righ ts Office and at the United Nations,\nL458: \nL459: have designed improved ethical policies and practices for bridging epistemic, power and\nL460: \nL461: privilege, cultural, and political differences that sci entists often are not trained to understand. Yet\nL462: \nL463: as Hardison and Williams show, the more s cientists understand the significance of the practice\nL464: \nL465: and renewal of Indigenous knowledges for Indigenous peoples’ own purposes of preparing for\nL466: climate change and protecting their ways of life (sometimes called the governance value of\nL467: \nL468: Indigenous knowledges xxiv), the more scientists will grasp richer senses of their responsibilities to\nL469: \nL470: work with Indigenous collaborators mutually instead of exploitatively.xxv\nL471: \nL472: 6\nL473: \nL474: I ndigenizing Futures\nL475: \nL476: The First Alaskan’s Institute, an Indigenous organization, includes as one of its slogans,\nL477: \nL478: “progress for the next 10,000 years,” referring to Indigenous Alaskans’ own histories of living in\nL479: that region for that long. Since Indigenous peoples in North America think at this scale, the t ime\nL480: \nL481: period of European, U.S., and Canadian colonialism, imperialism, and settlement appears very\nL482: \nL483: short and acutely disruptive. Indigenous conceptions of the future often present striking contrasts\nL484: \nL485: between deep Indigenous histories and the brief, but highly disruptive colonial, capitalist, and\nL486: \nL487: industrial period s. Moreover, many Indigenous histories are explicit about the fact that\nL488: Indigenous peoples, as collective actors, have also influenced local and regional environments.\nL489: \nL490: Many peoples’ calendars and seaso nal rounds explicitly demonstrate how Indigenous peoples,\nL491: \nL492: thro ugh practices such as burning and fishing, managed and maintained certain ecosystems.\nL493: \nL494: These ecosystems also changed through human interventions such as regional trading.\nL495: \nL496: A term like “anthropog enic” has very diverse meanings for Indigenous peoples, from\nL497: gradual changes, such as the adoption of new “relatives” (e.g. adoption of the horse in North\nL498: \nL499: America) to the shaping of habitats for certain plants and animals, to disruptive settler\nL500: \nL501: colonialism, such as practiced by Europeans arriving in North America. “Anthropogenic climate\nL502: \nL503: change” or “the Anthropocene,” then, are not precise enough terms for many Indigenous peoples,\nL504: \nL505: because they sound like all humans are implicated in and affected by coloniali sm, capitalism and\nL506: industrialization in the same ways.xxvi\nL507: \nL508: Davis and Todd argue that the Anthropocene is rooted in colonization. For colonialism\nL509: \nL510: has always included terraforming that tears apart what they call the “flesh”xxvii\nL511: \nL512: xxviii\nL513: \nL514: of human-\nL515: \nL516: nonhuman-ecological relationships. That colonizers today, from settlers to imperialists, are\nL517: \nL518: concerned about climate change, suggests that they are now being affected by the seismic waves\nL519: of massive ecosystem transformation that began over 500 ye ars ago. Mitchell cautions against\nL520: \nL521: “marking European colonization as a driving force of the Anthropocene,” because doing so may\nL522: \nL523: “naturalize” colonization. That is, the “risk of equating human forms of agency with ‘natural\nL524: \nL525: forces’ is that they come to be s een as inevitable, determinate and less contestable than ‘political\nL526: \nL527: forces’.” Mitchell points out that “the Anthropocene is not the product of ‘humanity’, but rather\nL528: particular segments of it.”xxix\nL529: \nL530: For Indi genous peoples, we do not tell our futures beginning from the position of concern\nL531: \nL532: with the Anthropocene as a hitherto unanticipated v ision of human i ntervention, which involves\nL533: \nL534: mass extinctions and the disappearance of certain ecosystems. For the colonial period already\nL535: \nL536: rendered comparable o utcomes that cost Indigenous peoples their reciprocal relationships with\nL537: \nL538: thousands of plants, animals, and ecosystems—m ost of which are not coming back. As Gross\nL539: claims, “Native A mericans h ave seen the end of their respect ive worlds… Just as importantly,\nL540: \nL541: though, Indians survived the apocalypse. This raises the further question, t hen, of what happens to\nL542: \nL543: a society that has gone through an apocalyptic event?”xxx\nL544: \nL545: S ome answers to Gross’ question lie in the work of Indigenous Clim ate Change Studies\nL546: \nL547: described already in this essay. Indigenous imaginations of our futures in relation to climate\nL548: change—the stuff of didactic science fiction—begin already with our living today in post-\nL549: \nL550: apocalypt ic situation. Had someone told our ancestors a story of what today’s times are like for\nL551: \nL552: Indigenous peoples, our ancestors would surely have thought they were hearing dystopian tales.\nL553: \nL554: For Indigenous peoples live in worlds so changed by colonialism, ca pitalism, and\nL555: \nL556: industrialization that our collective self-determination and agency are compromised to a degree\nL557: our ancestor s would have been haunted by. Indigenizing our futures involves our reflecting on\nL558: \nL559: why our ancestors’ would have thought today’s times are dystopian.\nL560: \nL561: In our case, reflecting on why our ancestors’ would have perceived the present as\nL562: \nL563: dystopian provides guidance on how to live under post-apocalyptic conditions. The Menominee\nL564: \nL565: 7\nL566: \nL567: Nation’s recent development of culturally, spiritually, and econom ically significant sustainable\nL568: forest was actually their response to the colonially-induced destruction of their relationships with\nL569: \nL570: many species. The transition to forestry involved envisioning and performing certain relationships\nL571: \nL572: and responsibilities that would have mattered to their ancestors—just now in relation to forest\nL573: \nL574: biodiversity. The Menominee’s relationships to the forest motivates their ongoing leadership in\nL575: \nL576: addressing climate change. xxxii\nL577: \nL578: xxxiii\nL579: \nL580: xxxi Indigenous climate justic e activism is also about performing\nL581: these ancestrally inspired visions, including the recent insistence by some leaders of the Standing\nL582: \nL583: Rock Sioux Tribe that their resistance to the Dakota Access Pipeline is primarily about prayer,\nL584: \nL585: ceremony, honoring their ancestors, and renewing their reci procal responsibilities with water.\nL586: \nL587: Indigenous climate change studies perform futurities that Indigenous persons can build\nL588: \nL589: on in generations to come. That is, our actions today are cyclical performances; they are guided\nL590: by our reflection on our ancestors’ perspectives and on our desire to be good ancestors ourselves\nL591: \nL592: to future generations. Wildcat calls this performance “indigenuity;” Kimmerer, “re turning the\nL593: \nL594: gift” (if we think broadly, in a multigenerational sense, about what Kimmerer means, here).xxxiv\nL595: \nL596: So for Indigenous peoples, “the Anthropocene epoch,” as a concept some people invoke often to\nL597: \nL598: envision the future, does not present us, at first glance, wit h the specter of unprecedented\nL599: changes. Ind igenous Climate Change Studies is a field that opens up our interpretations of our\nL600: \nL601: own histories and futurities, with the goal of supporting Indigenous capac ities to address climate\nL602: \nL603: change and the continuance of flourishing future generations.\nL604: \nL605: NOT ES\nL606: \nL607: i Joanna Harrington, \"Climate Change, Human Rights, and the Right to Be Cold,\" Fordham Envtl. L. Rev. 18 (2006);\nL608: \nL609: Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of the Flathead Reservation, Climate Change Strategic Plan (2013); Sara\nL610: Shepherd, \"Conference at Haskell Explores How Climate Change Affects American Indians \" Lawrence Journal-\nL611: \nL612: World, September 22 2016; M andaluyong Declaration, \"Mandaluyong Declaration of the Global Conference on\nL613: \nL614: Indigenous Women, Climate Change and Redd Plus,\" in Indigenous Women, Climate Change & Forests, ed. Tebtebba\nL615: \n"
